WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.731
One strong implementation pattern is to
make the data query express the caller's

2
00:00:04.811 --> 00:00:05.620
allowed scope.

3
00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.930
Instead of loading an arbitrary project by
ID and hoping a later branch remembers

4
00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:14.241
the tenant check, query for the project
whose ID matches and whose tenant ID comes

5
00:00:14.321 --> 00:00:16.374
from trusted authentication context.

6
00:00:16.454 --> 00:00:20.498
A missing result then means the
application has no accessible object for that

7
00:00:20.578 --> 00:00:21.574
subject and scope.

8
00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:25.076
Exact ORM method names vary, but the
security property is stable:

9
00:00:25.156 --> 00:00:29.076
client-controlled identity does not choose
the authorization boundary.

10
00:00:29.156 --> 00:00:32.889
This pattern is especially useful for
ownership and tenant isolation.

11
00:00:32.969 --> 00:00:37.199
Repository methods can require both the
object identifier and an authorization

12
00:00:37.279 --> 00:00:39.892
scope, or accept a predicate derived from
policy.

13
00:00:39.972 --> 00:00:44.328
Avoid generic findById helpers in
sensitive services when they erase the security

14
00:00:44.408 --> 00:00:44.905
context.

15
00:00:44.985 --> 00:00:49.154
If unrestricted lookup is genuinely
required for an administrative workflow,

16
00:00:49.234 --> 00:00:53.589
give it a distinct, narrowly used name and
require the stronger policy explicitly.

17
00:00:53.669 --> 00:00:56.718
The code review should make the
exceptional path obvious.

18
00:00:56.798 --> 00:00:58.975
Query scoping also belongs on mutations.

19
00:00:59.055 --> 00:01:03.348
An update or delete constrained only by
object ID can become a write-oriented BOLA

20
00:01:03.428 --> 00:01:06.104
even when the preceding read was scoped
correctly.

21
00:01:06.184 --> 00:01:10.103
Prefer a transaction or mutation predicate
that includes the same tenant,

22
00:01:10.183 --> 00:01:12.548
owner, membership, or version constraints.

23
00:01:12.628 --> 00:01:16.921
Check the affected-row result and avoid
performing a second unscoped lookup that

24
00:01:17.001 --> 00:01:18.867
changes which record is acted upon.

25
00:01:18.947 --> 00:01:22.307
Not every authorization rule fits into one
database predicate.

26
00:01:22.387 --> 00:01:25.187
Delegation, field-level views, state
transitions,

27
00:01:25.267 --> 00:01:29.746
and relationships spanning services may
require loading a scoped candidate and then

28
00:01:29.826 --> 00:01:31.506
evaluating an explicit policy.

29
00:01:31.586 --> 00:01:33.639
The safe sequence is still deliberate:

30
00:01:33.719 --> 00:01:36.955
establish the broadest enforceable scope
during selection,

31
00:01:37.035 --> 00:01:40.208
obtain the specific object, apply any
richer action policy,

32
00:01:40.288 --> 00:01:43.523
and perform the operation without losing
those constraints.

33
00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:45.967
Query scoping is not a substitute for
policy;

34
00:01:46.047 --> 00:01:50.278
it is a way to make common authorization
omissions harder to express and to reduce

35
00:01:50.358 --> 00:01:53.780
the amount of unauthorized data that
enters application memory.
